From d52d4dd9e37f1d31183387b507d8198b2c31e404 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 07:44:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] chainload: A/B fallback on iBoot's PMU boot-error count,
 optional watchdog

UNTESTED on hardware; compile-checked for CHAINLOADING=1 and plain builds.

A chainloading stage-1 now reads iBoot's legacy PMU scratchpad (primary
"pmu,spmi" node, info-leg_scrpad) before it chainloads. iBoot increments
boot_error_count when it finds boot_stage != 0 at power-on, i.e. when the
previous boot did not end in a clean shutdown/restart (Linux macsmc-reboot
writes 0 from its reboot notifier; hangs, watchdog bites, power-button holds
and panic restarts leave 0x2f/0x30). A non-zero count therefore means the
last attempt failed, and the slot named by the new variable

  chainload.fallback=<PARTUUID>;<path>

is tried first. Either slot that cannot be read is followed by the other
before falling back to the proxy.

  chainload.wdt=<secs>      arm the SoC watchdog (<= 170 s) right before the
                            hand-over and tell the next m1n1 to keep it armed
                            (" m1n1.wdt=keep" appended to its boot_args
                            cmdline); Linux apple_wdt sees RESET_EN and keeps
                            it fed. 0/absent = today's behaviour.
  chainload.pmu-clear=0     leave a non-zero count for Linux to report and
                            clear (default: stage-1 clears it after reading,
                            so a payload that dies before macsmc-reboot can
                            never walk iBoot into its "needs to be
                            reinstalled" recovery).

The choice and the raw bytes go to the next stage as chosen.m5pro,boot-slot
and chosen.m5pro,pmu-scrpad. Old stage-2 builds ignore nothing they need;
an old stage-1 ignores the new variables ("Unknown variable").

Image size is unchanged (0x420000 raw, text ends 0x83854 of 0x90000).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
